### SA-0 — Baseline audit

> Read `EXISTING_STANDARD` end to end. Produce: (a) an inventory of every claim it makes, each classified `token | rule | pattern | rationale | process | stale`; (b) every place it is ambiguous to a machine — "appropriate", "consistent", "as needed", "use good judgement" — quoted with its location; (c) everything it does not cover, checked against the §4 schema; (d) its structure as an outline, with an honest verdict on whether that structure serves an AI reader or a human skimmer.
>
> Correct nothing. You are mapping what exists and where it fails. Output per §3.

### SA-1 / SA-2 — Example projects (Figma)

> Analyse `PROJECT_<A|B>` exhaustively via the §2A Playwright protocol. This is Neo *applied to a real product*: you are reverse-engineering the language in use, not the system itself. Everything you find here is evidence of how the system is meant to be assembled — and, where it departs from Neo, evidence of either a genuine product need or drift.
>
> Walk **every page and every frame**, including hidden and off-canvas ones. For each screen record purpose, canvas width, grid, and component instances present.
>
> Extract, with node-level provenance on every value:
> 1. **Colour** — every fill, stroke, gradient, overlay and shadow colour as hex, plus the bound variable name where one exists. Flag hardcoded values (a smell). Group by the semantic role usage implies: surface, raised surface, border, primary action, destructive, warning, success, info, disabled, focus ring, scrim, text primary/secondary/tertiary/inverse/link. Record every mode.
> 2. **Typography** — every distinct text style: family, weight, size, line-height, letter-spacing, case, and what it is used for. Build the scale actually in use; list one-off sizes that break it.
> 3. **Spacing** — read gaps, paddings and margins from auto-layout frames. Derive the base unit and the real scale. Flag off-scale values.
> 4. **Layout** — page grids (columns, gutter, margin, type), container max-widths, nav/sidebar widths, content column widths, breakpoints implied by frame widths, header and footer heights, layering.
> 5. **Shape and depth** — radii per component class, border widths and colours, every shadow written out as full CSS.
> 6. **Components** — every instance: which library component, which variant/props, and every local override. Overrides are evidence the library is missing something; list them all.
> 7. **States** — hover, focus, disabled, loading, empty, error, skeleton. Explicitly name which are *absent* from the designs.
> 8. **Iconography and imagery** — icon set, sizes, stroke weight; image aspect ratios, crops, treatments, placeholder style; illustration or photography style.
> 9. **Motion** — every prototype interaction: trigger, animation, duration, easing.
> 10. **Content** — real microcopy: button labels, headings, empty states, errors. Capture voice: sentence vs title case, punctuation, person, verb style, product terminology.
> 11. **Density and rhythm** — vertical rhythm between sections, table row heights, field heights, tap target sizes.
> 12. **Accessibility** — compute contrast for every text-on-surface pair found; mark AA/AAA pass or fail. Note focus indicator treatment and minimum target sizes.
>
> Then a short section: **"What makes this app look like ours?"** — the three to five choices that carry the identity, as distinct from choices any competent designer would have made. Be specific, and be honest if the answer is "nothing distinctive".
>
> Output per §3. **SA-2 additionally:** diff project B against project A and classify every difference as *intentional product variation*, *drift*, or *contradiction*.

### SA-3 — Neo (the design system file)

> Analyse `FIGMA_NEO` via the §2A protocol. This is Neo, the design system itself, and it outranks both example projects.
>
> Extract: the **complete variable set** (every collection, every mode, every alias resolved to a primitive); the **complete style set** (colour, text, effect, grid); the **complete component set** — name, description, every property and its values, every variant permutation, base size, slots, published or not.
>
> Then answer explicitly:
> - What is the naming convention for variables, styles and components? State it as a rule someone could apply to name a new one.
> - Which Neo components are unused in either example project, and which project-level overrides indicate a component Neo is missing?
> - Which library values are *not* variables — raw values sitting in the library itself?
> - What is the theming model: how many modes, what switches them, what is themeable versus fixed?
>
> Output per §3, including the full token JSON.

### SA-4 — Storybook

> Analyse `STORYBOOK` completely. This is Neo rendered and documented — the closest thing to a spec that already exists, and the fastest route to the component API.
>
> Crawl **every story of every component**, not the docs landing pages. Per component record: name, the invocation as written in markup, the full controls/args table with types and defaults, every variant and size, what each individual story is demonstrating (stories are the design team's own statement of intended use — treat the set of stories as a list of sanctioned use cases), and any usage or accessibility notes in the docs pages.
>
> For each component, also capture from the rendered output: the DOM structure, the class names, the computed styles of the default state, and the actual behaviour of hover, focus, active, disabled, loading and error where the story exposes them. Use the browser for this — read computed styles, do not infer from screenshots.
>
> Then flag the gaps that matter to an agent:
> - Components present in Neo (SA-3) with no story, and stories with no Neo counterpart.
> - States the example projects use that no story demonstrates.
> - Anywhere the docs prose says something the controls table contradicts.
>
> Treat Storybook as authoritative for API and behaviour, but **subordinate to the shipped package** — it can lag a release. SA-5 settles any disagreement.
>
> Output per §3.

### SA-5 — The ProGet packages (shipped truth)

> Analyse the packages on `PROGET_FEED`. **This is the highest authority in the chain: what ships is what is true.**
>
> If `PACKAGES` says "discover from feed", enumerate the feed first and identify every package belonging to the design system — components, tokens/theme, icons, fonts, and anything they depend on. List what you found and what you judged out of scope, with reasoning. Record the latest stable version of each, and whether the example projects are pinned to older ones (if they are, say so loudly — it means SA-1/SA-2 describe an older design language).
>
> **Getting at the contents.** A `.nupkg` is a zip. Pull and unpack each one, and read all of it:
> - `<PackageId>.nuspec` — version, target frameworks, and the dependency graph between the design packages.
> - `lib/<tfm>/` — the assemblies; extract the public API surface.
> - `staticwebassets/`, `content/`, `contentFiles/` — **this is where the design lives.** Stylesheets, CSS custom properties, theme files, fonts, icon assets, JS.
> - `.razor` components and `.razor.css` scoped styles if these are Razor class libraries; any `.scss` sources or generated CSS.
>
> Record the consumer-facing asset paths **exactly** — for a Razor class library that means the `_content/<PackageId>/...` form. An agent has to type these, and a near-miss path silently ships an unstyled app.
>
> **Token layer.** Find the real token definitions — CSS custom properties, SCSS variables, a theme class, whatever form they take. Transcribe them completely and resolved. Record the exact syntax a consumer writes to reference a token, and how theming works: cascade order, which stylesheet must be registered first, and whether overriding is supported at all or is a violation.
>
> **Reconcile against SA-4 as you go.** Anywhere the shipped package differs from Storybook — a parameter that no longer exists, a renamed token, a changed default — the package wins and the difference is logged as a conflict.
>
> **Finally, the setup block.** Produce copy-pasteable code covering: package reference and feed configuration, service registration, stylesheet and script registration in the correct order, using/import statements, and theme initialisation. An agent starting from an empty project must be able to render one styled component from this block alone. If you cannot produce that with confidence, say so — it is the single highest-value thing in the whole standard.
>
> Output per §3.

### SA-6 — Reconciliation

> You have all prior findings files. Produce the single reconciled truth.
>
> 1. **Conflict matrix** — every property where sources disagree, all values, and the ruling under the order in §0, stated explicitly per row.
> 2. **Canonical token set** — §4 JSON, every value resolved, every value carrying provenance.
> 3. **Drift report** — three diffs, each with severity: Neo vs shipped package, Storybook vs shipped package, and each example project vs Neo. This goes to the design team; it does not belong in the standard.
> 4. **Gap list** — everything an agent will need that no source provides. Propose a defensible default for each, marked `PROPOSED — needs sign-off`.
> 5. **Invariants** — rules holding across Neo, the packages and both example projects. These become the MUSTs. Separate them from conventions that hold usually (SHOULD) and variation that is legitimately free (MAY).
>
> Output per §3 plus `WORK_DIR/findings/canonical-tokens.json`.

### SA-7 — Anti-AI-design-pattern research

> Research, using the web, what currently makes an interface read as machine-generated. Cite every source. Cover at minimum:
> - **Visual tells** — the specific palettes, gradients, typefaces, radii, shadow recipes, glassmorphism and hero compositions that recur in AI output regardless of brief.
> - **Structural tells** — three-card feature grids, symmetry everywhere, decorative 01/02/03 markers on things that are not sequences, emoji as iconography, every section on the same rhythm, centre-alignment by default.
> - **Copy tells** — "seamlessly", "effortlessly", "elevate", "unlock", feature-as-benefit phrasing, apologetic errors, vague empty states.
> - **Motion tells** — uniform fade-up-on-scroll, animation with no informational purpose.
> - **Absence tells** — no focus states, no loading states, no empty states, no error states, no considered dense-data view, optical alignment ignored, even spacing where hierarchy was needed.
>
> Research the inverse too: the specific, teachable choices that make an interface read as designed by a person with a point of view.
>
> The actual deliverable is the conversion of all that into **enforceable rules for our system**. Not "avoid generic gradients" but concrete banned values and constructions, each paired with the correct move inside our tokens:
>
> | Banned | Why it reads as AI | Do this instead (our tokens) |
>
> Cross-check every ban against SA-6's canonical tokens. If a banned pattern is something our design system genuinely does, **do not ban it** — record the tension for a human ruling. House style outranks a generic anti-AI heuristic every time.
>
> Output per §3.

### SA-8 — Visual specimens

> Using the image generation skill, produce the visual reference set. Every image derives strictly from `canonical-tokens.json` — no invented colours, sizes or components. Save to `WORK_DIR/assets/`.
>
> 1. Colour system sheet — every semantic role, every mode, with token name, hex and contrast rating.
> 2. Type scale specimen — every step at true size with token name and metrics.
> 3. Spacing and grid diagram — base unit, scale, and the layout grid at each breakpoint.
> 4. Component anatomy plates — the ten most-used components: labelled parts, all states in a row, correct sizes.
> 5. Layout archetype wireframes — the recurring page shapes across both example projects: list/table, detail, form, dashboard, empty, error.
> 6. Do/Don't pairs — one per high-value rule from SA-6 and SA-7. Correct left, the specific failure right, caption naming the rule.
