### SA-9 — Write the standard

> Write `WORK_DIR/DESIGN-STANDARD.md`. Complete rewrite, not an edit. One file. The reader is an autonomous coding agent with no prior knowledge of us and no way to ask a question.
>
> **Voice:** imperative and testable. Every rule uses MUST / MUST NOT / SHOULD / MAY and is falsifiable — a reviewer must be able to point at a screen and say the rule was broken. Delete every sentence that could not fail.
>
> **Structure:**
> 1. **How to use this document** — reading order, and the decision procedure for when the standard is silent.
> 2. **Agent brief** — the compressed version, under 1500 words, that fits in a code-gen system prompt: the token block, the MUSTs, the banned list, the checklist. Everything needed for a routine screen, on one screen.
> 3. **Foundations** — colour, type, space, layout, shape, elevation, motion, iconography. Each: tokens, rules for choosing between them, the specimen image, worked code in `TARGET_STACK`.
> 4. **Components** — the catalogue. Per component: when to use, when not to, API, every state, composition rules, accessibility obligations, correct code example. Include the forbidden-usage list from SA-6.
> 5. **Patterns** — assemblies above component level: page shells, forms and validation, tables and data density, navigation, modals and confirmation, notifications, loading, empty, error, permission-denied. Anatomy, rules, wireframe.
> 6. **Content and voice** — microcopy rules derived from real product copy, with a terminology table and before/after examples.
> 7. **Accessibility floor** — non-negotiables as a checklist, each with how to verify it.
> 8. **Anti-patterns** — SA-7's banned table plus our own drift examples.
> 9. **Self-verification checklist** — what an agent runs against its own output before declaring done. Every item binary and checkable, ideally by an automated probe.
> 10. **Appendices** — the full canonical token JSON as a fenced block, the provenance index, unresolved items, changelog.
>
> **Constraints:** no rule without a token or code reference behind it. No prose paragraph where a table works. Every image placed with alt text. Anything SA-6 marked `PROPOSED` is labelled inline as provisional. Where something is genuinely a judgement call, give the heuristic and the tiebreaker — never the word "appropriate".

### SA-10 — Adversarial verification

> You have seen no prior phase. You are given only `DESIGN-STANDARD.md` and the component package.
>
> Build three screens cold, in `TARGET_STACK`, against the real packages: (1) a data-dense list/table view with filters, (2) a multi-step form with validation and error states, (3) a detail page with a destructive action and confirmation. Use only what the standard tells you.
>
> Report, brutally:
> - Every point where you had to guess, and what you guessed.
> - Every contradiction between sections.
> - Every rule you could not verify you had followed.
> - Every unfalsifiable statement you found.
> - Screenshots of what you built, scored against the standard's own checklist and against the SA-7 banned list.
>
> The orchestrator feeds this report back to SA-9 for exactly one revision pass. Fix the gaps found; do not expand scope.

